;; The compile boundary on macOS — docs/goal/architecture/build-compile-cache.md
;; § The boundary's scope and what OFF means, decisions (1) and (7). Applied by
;; `sandbox-exec -f <this file> -D <role>=<path>…`: by the cargo boundary on every
;; compiling cargo (`scripts/cargo-boundary.py`), by the kache launcher's macOS tail
;; (a dev machine's installed copy beside the launcher, put there by
;; `just kache-launcher-install`), and on a hosted macOS CI runner, which reads this
;; copy from the tree. Every process they start inherits it.
;;
;; No network — an allowlist, never a list of what is refused (decision (1), the
;; socket-family paragraph): every outbound connection of every family is denied but
;; to a unix socket under the compile cache's store, so IPv4, IPv6, AF_VSOCK (the
;; hypervisor's transport) and every family XNU adds later are refused alike; the
;; compile cache's wrapper reaches its daemon through `<store>/daemon.sock`. Every
;; other unix socket is refused too (decision (8), darwin): a pathname socket is
;; reachable whatever the write rules say, and the session host's control socket
;; (`~/.cache/fauna/session-host/<session>.sock`) types into a live session —
;; measured 2026-11-05 on macOS: from inside, a listener at that path's shape connected
;; under the old `(remote unix-socket)` allow and answers EPERM under this one, the
;; store's socket connecting under both. An unused store role allows no unix connect
;; at all. The mDNSResponder socket's deny stays AFTER the allow (the last matching
;; rule wins), a belt should the store ever be named above it: with it gone a name
;; resolves (measured 2026-11-05), and a lookup carries data out. Creating a
;; socket is refused too for every family but unix: `system-socket` is not asked for
;; AF_INET/AF_INET6 (measured 2026-11-05: both open under the deny, as on Linux's
;; line; their connects are refused above), and refused for AF_VSOCK, AF_ROUTE,
;; AF_SYSTEM and AF_NDRV — `socket(AF_VSOCK)` answers EPERM, as Linux's line
;; answers EAFNOSUPPORT. Measured 2026-11-05 on a macOS the virtualization host guest: outside, a
;; vsock socket opens and its connect to the host (CID 2) reaches the transport
;; (EAGAIN); under the old `(remote ip)` deny the same; under this profile both EPERM.
;;
;; No write but the writable set: everything on disk stays readable, and a write is
;; allowed only under the path each role parameter names — the target cargo resolves,
;; this compile's scratch (`TMPDIR`, a directory under the target), the compile
;; cache's store, kache's state directory (`~/Library/Caches/kache`: its probe,
;; rustc, sysroot and linker digests) and its log directory (`~/Library/Logs/kache`,
;; its `kache.log` — refused otherwise, measured 2026-11-05), and the workspace root
;; for `cargo fix` / `clippy --fix`. Every role is passed on every
;; line (an unset one fails the profile closed); an unused one names
;; `/dev/null/fauna-unused-role`, which cannot exist. So nothing a build script or
;; proc-macro writes is run later outside: not the rustup proxy, the cargo shim, the
;; helper, `just`, this profile, nor a runner's file-command files.
;;
;; The writes a compile makes outside its set, measured on macOS 2026-11-05 under this
;; profile without the device allows (cold `cargo check -p fauna-core`, macOS and
;; aarch64-apple-ios-sim, every refusal read from the kernel's sandbox report):
;;   /dev/null          — allowed: build scripts open it for writing to discard a
;;                        probe's output (rustix's build script fails without it).
;;   /dev/dtracehelper  — allowed: dyld registers every process's DTrace probes there
;;                        at launch; refused, nothing fails, but every process of the
;;                        compile files a report.
;;   ~/.cargo/.package-cache, .package-cache-mutate, .global-cache, the registry's
;;                        fileprovider xattr — DENIED: cargo's home is read-only by
;;                        the ruling; cargo carries on without its locks after the
;;                        `cargo fetch` prelude outside, as on Linux.
;;   $DARWIN_USER_TEMP_DIR/xcrun_db-*, $DARWIN_USER_CACHE_DIR/…/PlugInCache-xcodebuild
;;                        — DENIED: xcrun's SDK lookup cache, read later by every xcrun
;;                        outside; a compile that could write it could point the next
;;                        tool lookup at a file in its target. xcrun answers uncached.
;;   /dev/tty           — DENIED: nothing a compile needs; refused, nothing fails.
;;
;; And one write by vnode type, not by path: DATA into an EXISTING FIFO. A grant's
;; jobserver is a FIFO in the build slot's directory, outside every role, named to
;; cargo by `CARGO_MAKEFLAGS=--jobserver-auth=fifo:<path>`; cargo and rustc open it
;; read-write to take and return tokens. Refused, cargo warns `failed to connect to
;; jobserver … Operation not permitted` and runs at its own width — the mac job pool's
;; join undone silently (measured on macOS 2026-11-05: a cold three-crate check
;; against an empty jobserver FIFO). Allowed: opening an existing FIFO for writing. Still refused
;; under it (measured the same day): creating a FIFO, unlinking one, and every
;; regular-file write. It is the Linux line's reading too — a read-only bind refuses
;; a write to a regular file, never to a FIFO.
;;
;; No network through a system service either (decision (1), the darwin hole): a
;; process may hand network work to an XPC service running outside this profile — a
;; background URLSession's transfer is nsurlsessiond's, measured reaching a loopback
;; server and the internet and bringing a body back in. So every mach-lookup is
;; refused but a list of literal global names, each with who asks for it and why it
;; stays; no pattern, no prefix, no local name. The list was cut by a deny-run on
;; macOS 2026-11-06 (cold `cargo check -p fauna-core`, macOS and aarch64-apple-ios-sim,
;; kache-wrapped and FAUNA_KACHE=0, and `-p fauna-ffi` for ios-sim kache-wrapped; each
;; green with the same artifacts as under every name allowed): a name stays only when
;; a tool fails or changes its result without it, or every process asks for it at
;; launch so a refusal files a report per process.
;;   com.apple.logd, com.apple.system.notification_center — KEPT: every Apple tool
;;                        asks for both at launch (sandbox-exec, cargo, rustup, cc,
;;                        clang, ar, ld, xcrun, xcodebuild — rustc, kache, build
;;                        scripts and proc-macros look nothing up); refused, nothing fails,
;;                        but every process files two reports.
;; Dropped — refused, nothing fails, the compile's artifacts the same:
;;   com.apple.system.opendirectoryd.libinfo (cargo, rustup, xcodebuild, the kache
;;                        daemon: user lookups, which fall back to HOME) and .membership (cc, clang,
;;                        ar, dsymutil, git, xcrun, xcodebuild, which then ask
;;                        com.apple.bsd.dirhelper, refused too: confstr cannot name
;;                        DARWIN_USER_TEMP_DIR, each tool warns on stderr and uses /tmp,
;;                        and xcrun answers the same but uncached);
;;                        com.apple.cfprefsd.agent and .daemon
;;                        (preferences go unread; no report — CoreFoundation checks the
;;                        sandbox before it looks up); com.apple.SystemConfiguration.configd
;;                        (cargo, rustup, the kache daemon); com.apple.distributed_notifications@Uv3 and
;;                        com.apple.CoreServices.coreservicesd (cargo, xcodebuild; cargo
;;                        then asks com.apple.DiskArbitration.diskarbitrationd, refused
;;                        too); com.apple.metadata.mds (cargo: Spotlight);
;;                        com.apple.FSEvents and com.apple.lsd.mapdb (xcodebuild, for
;;                        xcrun's SDK lookup, which then asks com.apple.lsd.modifydb,
;;                        refused too); com.apple.analyticsd (ld, codesign: telemetry);
;;                        com.apple.security.syspolicy (codesign) — each files a
;;                        report from the tool that asks.
;; A name a later toolchain or a hosted runner's image needs is added here BY NAME with
;; its reason, read from the kernel's sandbox report, never by widening. The deny of
;; com.apple.nsurlsessiond after the allow is a belt (the last matching rule wins),
;; should a wide allow ever be written above it, as the mDNSResponder socket's is.
;; And cfprefsd writes ~/Library/Preferences on a caller's behalf, outside the writable
;; set: a preference set from inside landed there under (allow default) (measured
;; 2026-11-06); user-preference-write is refused, and a domain a compile's tool needs
;; to write would be allowed by its literal preference-domain.
(version 1)
(allow default)
(deny network-outbound)
(allow network-outbound (remote unix-socket (subpath (param "FAUNA_STORE"))))
(deny network-outbound (literal "/private/var/run/mDNSResponder"))
(deny system-socket)
(allow system-socket (socket-domain AF_UNIX))
(deny mach-lookup)
(allow mach-lookup
  (global-name "com.apple.logd")
  (global-name "com.apple.system.notification_center"))
(deny mach-lookup (global-name "com.apple.nsurlsessiond"))
(deny user-preference-write)
(deny file-write*)
(allow file-write*
  (subpath (param "FAUNA_TARGET"))
  (subpath (param "FAUNA_SCRATCH"))
  (subpath (param "FAUNA_STORE"))
  (subpath (param "FAUNA_KACHE_STATE"))
  (subpath (param "FAUNA_KACHE_LOG"))
  (subpath (param "FAUNA_WORKSPACE")))
(allow file-write-data
  (literal "/dev/null")
  (literal "/dev/dtracehelper"))
(allow file-write-data (vnode-type FIFO))
